course: dictionary
lesson: 9285
title: Sparks and electricity
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spark is a tiny bright flash. It can be a bit of fire that flies off a fire, or a quick jump of electricity across a gap. The sentences around the word give you the clue: when you read «a spark» near words like electricity, gap, or flash, it means the bright jumping kind.
explain: «When enough charge builds up it can jump across a gap as a spark, like the tiny zap from a door handle after you shuffle across the carpet.» Here the clues are charge, jump and zap. Try the meaning in the sentence: a flash of electricity jumping across a gap fits, so that is the right meaning.
explain: Spark also works as a verb, meaning to start something. «Memes and comedy can spark curiosity» means they can start people wanting to know more. In a story, a spark of an idea is a small start that sets the adventure going.
fact: A spark is a tiny bright bit of fire or burning material that flies off.
fact: A spark is a quick flash of electricity that jumps across a gap.
fact: To spark something means to start it, like spark an idea or spark an interest.
fact: A spark of something means a small amount of it, like a spark of hope.
fact: A lightning rod gives lightning an easy metal path into the ground, so it does not go through the building.
fact: Sparks can start fires, so keep away from sparking sockets and always tell an adult.
exercise: «Memes and comedy can spark curiosity, but you need to know the real story to understand the joke.» What does spark mean in this sentence? | To be very tired | *To start something, like a feeling of wanting to know | To put out a fire | To squash something flat
exercise: «A lightning rod gives that spark an easy metal path down into the earth.» What is the spark in this sentence? | A loud song | A piece of wood | *A quick flash of electricity jumping across a gap | A bit of cooked food
exercise: What does «a spark of hope» mean? | *A small amount of hope | A huge pile of hope | A loud shout of joy | A dark, sad feeling
test: «First huge presses squash the steel into doors and roofs, then swarms of robot arms join the pieces while sparks fly.» What are the sparks here? | Robot arms moving slowly | Large clouds of smoke | *Tiny bright bits of hot material flying off | Pieces of cold steel
test: «A girl in Texas put a metal spoon in a microwave; it sparked and caught fire.» Why is this a warning? | Metal spoons make food taste better | Microwaves only work with spoons | *Sparks from metal in a microwave can start a fire, so never do it | Sparks are a fun game to watch
exam: Why must you keep away from sparking sockets and tell an adult? | *Sparks from a damaged socket can give a dangerous shock or start a fire | They are a fun game to play with | They are warm and cosy to sit near | They are only for lights and are safe to touch
